A||||7||1986 Fiero||Very low oil pressure???||||5||
Z||000000||1986 Fiero||07-22-2001||11:47 AM||||I just bought a 1985 gt 2.8 V6 at idle is has almost no oil pressure the gauge shows almost in the red...and while i am going down the road i only get about 40 pounds of pressure.this happened after i changed the oil.....What should i do.....||||reg||5
Z||000001||maryjane||07-22-2001||11:55 AM||||More info please. What was the oil press before?<BR>what oil did you use before?<BR>What kind of oil did you go to?<BR>Synthetic? blend?<BR>What vis or wt?<BR>How many miles on eng?<BR>Exactly how soon after oil chng did problem start.<BR>Coolant temp ok?<BR>I probably won't know the solution, but those that may will need to know these things. good luck<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||07-22-2001||06:01 PM||||I think the first thing you should do is check the actual pressure by hooking up a mechanical gauge in place of the sending unit and see what the pressure actually is.  The sending units in these cars often fails and/or reads very inaccurately. <P>I replaced the sending unit on my blue 87 GT and the gauge now reads about 10-15 lbs higher than it was.  The warning light does blink on at hot idle any more.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||07-22-2001||11:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bob Englert:<BR><B>I think the first thing you should do is check the actual pressure by hooking up a mechanical gauge in place of the sending unit and see what the pressure actually is.  The sending units in these cars often fails and/or reads very inaccurately. <P>I replaced the sending unit on my blue 87 GT and the gauge now reads about 10-15 lbs higher than it was.  The warning light does blink on at hot idle any more.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>&gt;That's a very good idea.  I'm hoping that's what's wrong with mine.  It goes from 65 to 18 in a matter of a few minutes.  The oil light comes on at times as well, unless I tach it up.  Mine has 64K on it, 2.8L.  I'll have to find out soon.  Thanks.<P>BTW:  How much did this sending unit cost?  I work @ a mechanic's shop, so I could get the part for cost and put it in myself.  Thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||07-23-2001||12:15 AM||||I'm sure this is probably unrelated since I'm uncertain if the 2.8 has an oil bypass in the pump (where it bypasses the whole system and dumps back into the oil sump) but a number of years ago I had an Opel GT that did something similar to me, at idle I had about 10 PSI and at highway speeds (tach reading about 4K) it was closer to about 40, and turned out that the spring for the bypass was a bit weak and allowed the bypass to hold open thereby reducing my oil pressure severely. fortunately the bypass was accessable from the exterior of the timing cover so I didn't have to tear into the motor. But might be something to check into if the pressure is in fact that low when tested with a known working gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||07-23-2001||06:38 AM||||When I first got my 85 I changed the oil and put in the 10w30 that the book said to use, the car had low oil pressure and it leaked real bad. The sending unit was leaking and I was out of oil in two days, I replaced the sending unit (AutoZone) and filled her up with some 10w40 and now have great oil pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fropuff||07-23-2001||08:51 PM||||I think I'll just buy the sending unit w/o testing it.  They're probably not that expensive.  Any part #s?  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||07-23-2001||08:56 PM||||GM p/n 10045775.  You really do want to check the pressure with a mechanical gauge though. <BR>||||reg||1
